The question asks what this PHP code will print:

$x = 5;
$f = function () use ($x) { return $x; };
$x = 7;
echo $f();

In PHP, when you use `use ($x)` in a closure without `&`, the variable is passed by value at the time the closure is created. So `$x` is captured as 5. Even though `$x` is later changed to 7, the closure still holds the original value of 5.

So the output will be 5.

The statement says "The instanceof operator only checks class inheritance: it ignores interfaces."

In PHP, the `instanceof` operator checks if an object is an instance of a specific class OR implements a specific interface. It does NOT ignore interfaces. For example:

```php
class Foo implements Countable {
    public function count(): int { return 1; }
}
$f = new Foo();
var_dump($f instanceof Countable); // true
```

So the statement is false - instanceof does check interfaces as well as class inheritance.

The question asks what this PHP code will print:

$a = [1, 2];
$b = $a;
